Janis Papanagnou wrote:
> Nephi schrieb:
>> It has been proposed many times that mirrors should provide
>> reflection. Of course, given the prevalence of nymphs, that would be
>> unbalancing. But it seems to me that if a wielded mirror gave
>> reflection, but shattered after a single use (or perhaps had a
>> significant chance of shattering after each use), game balance would
>> be maintained. It would give games a little more flavor, I think.
> 
> I think a mirror should reflect _without breaking_, as long as the
> mirror is _explicitly wielded_. In practise that bears the danger
> of hitting a monster with the wielded mirror by accident (thereby
> imposing a severe luck penalty). A handicap is that you first have
> to wield it in case of breath/ray attacks, usually (in case of rays)
> suffering from the first hit anyway, and switch again to your melee
> weapon (thus risking being zapped again, as long as the wand isn't
> empty) or giving you a chance to flee; I find this a quite balanced
> response to the mostly inevitable deadly wand attacks in the early
> game; you suffer, but you have a chance to survive (for the moment).
> 
> Janis

It should probably be a two-handed weapon.  Otherwise it raises the
question of why you can't use it like a shield of reflection: hold it
in your offhand and only attack with the primary weapon.  Allowing
that would be overpowered, but not allowing it if it's a one-handed
weapon seems arbitrary and counter to Nethack's "if it makes sense,
try it and discover that the DevTeam thought of it first" philosophy.
Worse still, a wizard could wield Magicbane in one hand, a mirror in
the other, and use thrown daggers and spells to attack.  The need to
switch weapons before making a melee attack would be well worth
getting what amounts to a shield of reflection that doesn't affect
spellcasting in the early game.

Alternately, just make it so a successful *melee* attack *against* the
wielder has a chance of breaking the mirror.  The player wouldn't
suffer a luck penalty, since the monster broke it, but the item is
still destroyed.  This offers a disincentive to keep it wielded at
all times, even for players who don't use melee predominantly.


